There is no application code in this directory (it only holds Claude config), so this is a standalone sketch in TypeScript, which is my guess at your stack. `fetchUserProfile` and `UserProfile` stand in for your existing call and type.

```ts
const TTL_MS = 60_000;
const cache = new Map<string, { at: number; value: Promise<UserProfile> }>();

export function getUserProfile(id: string): Promise<UserProfile> {
  const hit = cache.get(id);
  if (hit && Date.now() - hit.at < TTL_MS) return hit.value;

  // Cache the promise so concurrent callers share one request.
  const value = fetchUserProfile(id);
  cache.set(id, { at: Date.now(), value });
  // Don't keep failures around for the full TTL.
  value.catch(() => {
    if (cache.get(id)?.value === value) cache.delete(id);
  });
  return value;
}

// Call this from every code path that updates a profile.
export function invalidateUserProfile(id: string): void {
  cache.delete(id);
}
```

Security warning: the cache is keyed by profile ID only, so if the response varies by who is asking (private fields, permissions), add the viewer's ID to the key or one user will be served another's view.

I left out two things:
- **Size bound:** expired entries are only replaced when the same ID is requested again, so memory grows with the number of distinct IDs. Add an LRU cap if that set is large.
- **Shared store:** the cache is per process, so with several server instances an update on one leaves stale data on the others for up to 60 seconds. Move it to Redis if that matters.

